<header><p class="st-kicker">other · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Mengjiang</h1><p class="st-meta">1936 CE – 1945 CE · capital Kalgan (Zhangjiakou)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Japanese-sponsored Mongol autonomous state in Inner Mongolia under Prince Demchugdongrub, which ruled from Kalgan until 1945.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demchugdongrub">Demchugdongrub</a> (De Wang) led an Inner Mongolian movement for autonomy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> for support. A Mongol Military Government was formed in 1936. After Japanese forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chahar-province">Chahar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suiyuan">Suiyuan</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> in 1937, three client governments were set up, and in 1939 they merged as the Mengjiang United Autonomous Government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhangjiakou">Kalgan</a> (Zhangjiakou).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Japanese advisers controlled policy, but the state allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> a voice in their own affairs and promoted Mongolian language, schools and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> alongside a much larger Chinese farming population. Its economy supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, iron ore and other resources to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. From 1940 it was nominally part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wang-jingwei-regime">Wang Jingwei regime</a> while remaining autonomous, and in 1941 it was restyled the Mongolian Autonomous Federation.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Soviet and Mongolian forces invaded in August 1945, and the state collapsed; the region later became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demchugdongrub">Demchugdongrub</a> later fled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolian-peoples-republic">Mongolian People's Republic</a>, was handed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and imprisoned until 1963.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mengjiang.jpg" alt="Mengjiang" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: EuropeanV · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mengjiang.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1936 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kalgan (Zhangjiakou)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Demchugdongrub (De Wang), Yondonwangchug</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism, Chinese folk religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mongolian, Mandarin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mengkiang, Mongol United Autonomous Government</dd></dl></aside></div>

